    {
      "short": "RBAC",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Roles",
      "body": [
        "Inside a firm, people need different powers. With <span class=\"term\">role-based access control</span> (RBAC), users get roles — owner, accountant, viewer — and each role maps to a set of permissions like <code>invoices:delete</code>.",
        "Cal is a viewer, so <code>DELETE /invoices/1042</code> returns 403. RBAC is easy to reason about and audit: \"who can delete invoices?\" is one lookup. Code should check permissions, not role names, so roles can change without code changes."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "+ Roles: owner, accountant, viewer",
        "+ Permission check before each action"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "exposed": "no",
        "checks": "per role",
        "privilege": "—"
      },
