    {
      "short": "ABAC",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Rules about attributes",
      "body": [
        "Then the rules get specific: approvers may approve invoices from <i>their own department</i>, under <i>$5,000</i>. Roles alone would need a role per department per limit — a <span class=\"term\">role explosion</span>.",
        "<span class=\"term\">Attribute-based access control</span> (ABAC) decides with rules over attributes of the user, the resource and the context. Dev's $48,000 Ops invoice is over his limit and is refused. Most real systems combine both: roles for the broad strokes, attributes for the fine print."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "+ Rule: same department, under $5,000",
        "~ Over-limit approval refused"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "exposed": "no",
        "checks": "roles + attributes",
        "privilege": "—"
      },
      "scene": {
        "entities": {
          "ana": {
            "type": "device",
            "x": -15,
            "y": -26,
            "w": 5,
            "d": 5,
            "label": "Ana · approver, Sales"
          },
          "bo": {
            "type": "device",
            "x": 5,
            "y": -28,
            "w": 5,
            "d": 5,
            "label": "Dev · approver, Ops"
          },
          "api": {
            "type": "block",
